2186087353 1078 #edb/ref 17592186432534 13194139534682 17592186045417 true] [17592186087601 1074 "**Step 1:** First we find the complementary solution $y_c$ by solving the homogeneous ODE. The characteristic equation\n\n$$\n\\lambda^2 - 4\\lambda + 4 = 0\n$$\n\nhas a repeated root $\\lambda =  2,$ so the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c = A e ^ { 2 x} + B x e ^ {2 x}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Second, we find a particular solution $y_p$ of the inhomogeneous ODE. Since the right hand side is a polynomial of degree $1,$ a particular solution will also be a polynomial of degree $1.$ So we assume\n\n$$\ny_p=\\alpha x + \\beta\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of $y_p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y_p}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\alpha,\\qquad \\frac{\\textrm{d}^2y_p}{\\textrm{d}x^2} = 0.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta,$ we substitute the derivatives into the original ODE and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0-4\\alpha +4(\\alpha x+\\beta ) & =8x+4 \\\\ 4\\alpha x+(4\\beta -4\\alpha ) & =8x+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}4\\alpha =8\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 4\\beta -4\\alpha =4\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha=2, \\beta=3.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution $y_p$ is\n\n$$\ny_p =2x+3.\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Finally, we write the general solution as the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={y}_{c}+{y}_{p} \\\\  & =A{e}^{2x}+Bx{e}^{2x}+2x+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534682 17592186045417 true] [17592186087601 1074 "**Step 1:** First we find the complementary solution $y_c$ by solving the homogeneous ODE. The characteristic equation\n\n$$\n\\lambda^2 - 4\\lambda + 4 = 0\n$$\n\nhas a repeated root $\\lambda =  2,$ so the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c = A e ^ { 2 x} + B x e ^ {2 x}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Second, we find a particular solution $y_p$ of the inhomogeneous ODE. Since the right hand side is a polynomial of degree $1,$ a particular solution will also be a polynomial of degree $1.$ So we assume\n\n$$\ny_p=\\alpha x + \\beta\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of $y_p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y_p}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\alpha,\\qquad \\frac{\\textrm{d}^2y_p}{\\textrm{d}x^2} = 0.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta,$ we substitute the derivatives into the original ODE and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0-4\\alpha +4(\\alpha x+\\beta ) & =8x+4 \\\\ 4\\alpha x+(4\\beta -4\\alpha ) & =8x+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}4\\alpha =8\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 4\\beta -4\\alpha =4\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha=2, \\beta=3.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution $y_p$ is\n\n$$\ny_p =2x+3.\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Finally, we write the general solution as the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={y}_{c}+{y}_{p} \\\\  & =A{e}^{2x}+Bx{e}^{2x}+2x+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534682 17592186045417 false] [17592186087602 1074 "**Step 1:** First we find the complementary solution $y_c$ by solving the homogeneous ODE. The characteristic equation\n\n$$\n\\lambda^2 + \\lambda - 2 = 0\n$$\n\nhas roots $\\lambda = 1,-2,$ so the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c = Ae^{x} + Be^{- 2 x}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Second, we find a particular solution $y_p$ of the inhomogeneous ODE. Since the right hand side is a polynomial of degree $2,$ a particular solution will also be a polynomial of degree $2.$ So we assume\n\n$$\ny_p =\\alpha x^2 + \\beta x + \\gamma\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha,$ $\\beta,$ and $\\gamma$ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of $y_p,$ we get\n\n$$\ny_p' = 2\\alpha x\n+\\beta,\\qquad y_p'' = 2\\alpha.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha,$ $\\beta,$ and $\\gamma,$ we substitute the derivatives into the original ODE and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\alpha +(2\\alpha x+\\beta )-2(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma ) & ={x}^{2}+5x-3 \\\\ -2\\alpha {x}^{2}+(2\\alpha -2\\beta )x+(2\\alpha +\\beta -2\\gamma ) & ={x}^{2}+5x-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}-2\\alpha =1\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 2\\alpha -2\\beta =5\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 2\\alpha +\\beta -2\\gamma =-3\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha=-\\dfrac{1}{2}, \\beta=-3, \\gamma=-\\dfrac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p =- \\frac{x^2}{2} - 3 x - \\dfrac 1 2.\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** We write the general solution as the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={y}_{c}+{y}_{p} \\\\  & =A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{-2x}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-3x-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534682 17592186045417 true] [17592186087602 1074 "**Step 1:** First we find the complementary solution $y_c$ by solving the homogeneous ODE. The characteristic equation\n\n$$\n\\lambda^2 + \\lambda - 2 = 0\n$$\n\nhas roots $\\lambda = 1,-2,$ so the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c = Ae^{x} + Be^{- 2 x}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Second, we find a particular solution $y_p$ of the inhomogeneous ODE. Since the right hand side is a polynomial of degree $2,$ a particular solution will also be a polynomial of degree $2.$ So we assume\n\n$$\ny_p =\\alpha x^2 + \\beta x + \\gamma\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha,$ $\\beta,$ and $\\gamma$ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of $y_p,$ we get\n\n$$\ny_p' = 2\\alpha x\n+\\beta,\\qquad y_p'' = 2\\alpha.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha,$ $\\beta,$ and $\\gamma,$ we substitute the derivatives into the original ODE and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\alpha +(2\\alpha x+\\beta )-2(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma ) & ={x}^{2}+5x-3 \\\\ -2\\alpha {x}^{2}+(2\\alpha -2\\beta )x+(2\\alpha +\\beta -2\\gamma ) & ={x}^{2}+5x-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-2\\alpha =1\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 2\\alpha -2\\beta =5\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 2\\alpha +\\beta -2\\gamma =-3\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha=-\\dfrac{1}{2}, \\beta=-3, \\gamma=-\\dfrac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p =- \\frac{x^2}{2} - 3 x - \\dfrac 1 2.\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** We write the general solution as the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={y}_{c}+{y}_{p} \\\\  & =A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{-2x}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-3x-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534682 17592186045417 false] [17592186087613 1074 "We find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(0) = -\\dfrac{1}{2}$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nA+B -\\dfrac{1}{2}  = -\\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition $y'(0) = 0,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\ny' = A e^{x} -2 B e^{-2 x} -x -3,\n$$\n\nand then we substitute $y'(0)=0$ into the above to get\n\n$$\nA-2B -3 = 0.\n$$\n\nSo we have the following system of equations for $A$ and $B\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}A+B=0 \\\\ A-2B=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=1, B=-1.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny = e^{x} -e^{-2 x}  - \\frac{x^2}{2} - 3 x - \\dfrac 1 2.\n$$" 13194139534682 17592186045417 true] [17592186087613 1074 "We find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(0) = -\\dfrac{1}{2}$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nA+B -\\dfrac{1}{2}  = -\\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition $y'(0) = 0,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\ny' = A e^{x} -2 B e^{-2 x} -x -3,\n$$\n\nand then we substitute $y'(0)=0$ into the above to get\n\n$$\nA-2B -3 = 0.\n$$\n\nSo we have the following system of equations for $A$ and $B\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}A+B=0 \\\\ A-2B=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=1, B=-1.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny = e^{x} -e^{-2 x}  - \\frac{x^2}{2} - 3 x - \\dfrac 1 2.\n$$" 13194139534682 17592186045417 false] [17592186087873 1038 #edb/ref 17592186432548 13194139534682 17592186045417 true] [17592186087873 1038 #edb/ref 17592186432552 13194139534682 17592186045417 true] [17592186087874 1038 #edb/ref 17592186432553 13194139534682 17592186045417 true] [17592186087875 1038 #edb/ref 17592186432550 13194139534682 17592186045417 true] [17592186087876 1038 #edb/ref 17592186432549 13194139534682 17592186045417 true] [17592186087876 1038 #edb/ref 17592186432551 13194139534682 17592186045417 true] [17592186087946 1050 1020.0 13194139534682 17592186045417 true] [17592186088228 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534682 17592186045417 true] [17592186088228 1071 "Given that the differential equation\n$\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} - 2\\frac{dy}{dx} - 8y =-16$\nhas the complementary function $y_{c}(x) = Ae^{4x} + Be^{-2x}$, find the particular solution $y_{p}(x)$ to this equation." 13194139534682 17592186045417 false] [17592186088228 1071 "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-8y=-16\n$$\n\nhas the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{4x}+B{e}^